    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#liu-bang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liu-bang.jpg" alt="Liu Bang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#liu-bang">Liu Bang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liu-bang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Han Dynasty emerged from the violent collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qin-dynasty">Qin Empire</a>, which had unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> in 221 BCE but destroyed itself within fifteen years through administrative brutality and punishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#corvee-labour">corvée</a> labour demands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>, a minor Qin official of peasant origin, was made King of Han in 206 BCE, outmanoeuvred his aristocratic rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chu-han-contention">Chu-Han War</a>, and after victory at Gaixia proclaimed himself emperor in 202 BCE, ruling as Emperor Gaozu. His founding genius was deliberate moderation: lowering the land tax to one-fifteenth of the harvest, later reduced to one-thirtieth, demobilising soldiers and labourers, and dismantling the Qin's most oppressive legal apparatus. Early Han rulers embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huang-lao-taoism">Huang-Lao</a> Taoist principles of minimal government intervention, allowing the population and economy to recover from decades of warfare. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> (r. 141–87 BCE), the dynasty's most transformative ruler, reversed this restraint, elevating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#confucianism">Confucianism</a> as state orthodoxy, establishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#imperial-academy">Imperial Academy</a> to train officials in the classical texts, and launching expansionist campaigns that vastly enlarged the empire. The Han inherited the Qin's unification but replaced its coercive model with an ideological one, creating a governing framework durable enough to survive Wang Mang's usurpation and reconstitution as the Eastern Han in 25 CE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#yangtze-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yangtze-river.jpg" alt="Yangtze River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dusk_on_the_Yangtze_River.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrew Hitchcock, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> recorded a population of nearly 60 million in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#census">census</a> of 2 CE. Agriculture was the economic foundation, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet">millet</a>, wheat, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a> cultivation across the Yellow and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> valleys generating the surplus that funded the imperial state. Technological advances including the iron plough, ox-drawn ploughing, and improved irrigation substantially increased yields. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">Silk</a> production was a major export industry, with Chinese silk reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> through Parthian and other intermediaries along the Central Asian routes opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>'s reign. The state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#salt-and-iron-monopoly">monopolies over salt and iron</a>, introduced under Emperor Wu to fund military campaigns, generated substantial imperial income while suppressing private competition, and were fiercely contested at the court Debate on Salt and Iron of 81 BCE. A merchant class existed but occupied a legally inferior social position below farmers in the Confucian hierarchy, subject to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumptuary-law">sumptuary</a> restrictions and periodic state predation, a structural tension between commercial vitality and ideological suspicion of trade that characterised Chinese political economy for centuries afterwards.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#emperor-wu-of-han"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han.jpg" alt="Emperor Wu of Han" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%BC%A2%E6%AD%A6%E5%B8%9D.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Han military combined a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#conscription">conscript</a> infantry base with a professional cavalry arm developed specifically to counter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> nomadic confederation, the empire's most persistent strategic threat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>'s campaigns against the Xiongnu, launched from 129 BCE onwards, were the dynasty's most sustained and expensive military effort, eventually fragmenting the confederation at a cost that destabilised imperial finances. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wei-qing">Wei Qing</a> and the cavalry commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huo-qubing">Huo Qubing</a> conducted the decisive northern campaigns, culminating in 119 BCE, that drove Xiongnu power beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gobi-desert">Gobi Desert</a>. Han armies also expanded southward, conquering Nanyue in 111 BCE, northeast into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#korea">Korea</a> in 108 BCE, and westward into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a> as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ferghana-valley">Ferghana Valley</a>, outflanking the Xiongnu by securing allied states along their western flank. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crossbow">crossbow</a>, fitted with standardised bronze trigger mechanisms, was the Han infantry's primary technological advantage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#military-colony">Military colonies</a>, garrisoned agricultural settlements along the northern frontier, provided a sustainable logistical model for holding conquered territory without continuous resupply from the interior.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#imperial-examination"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imperial-examination.jpg" alt="Imperial examination" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#imperial-examination">Imperial examination</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ming-Beamtenpr%C3%BCfungen1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Qiu Ying (仇英) (attributed), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Han administrative system established the template of Chinese imperial governance that persisted until 1912. The empire was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#commandery">commanderies</a> administered by centrally appointed officials, and after the Rebellion of the Seven States in 154 BCE, Emperors Jing and Wu progressively reduced the autonomous kingdoms to centrally controlled units. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#imperial-academy">Imperial Academy</a>, established in 124 BCE, trained officials in the Confucian classics and, together with local recommendation of candidates for their filial piety and integrity, supplied the bureaucracy with a theoretically meritocratic recruitment pipeline, a precursor of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#imperial-examination">examination system</a>. Han law built on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qin-dynasty">Qin</a> statutes but was moderated by reforms such as Emperor Wen's abolition of mutilating punishments in 167 BCE, producing a more measured framework than the legalist system it replaced. The Imperial Censor and inspectors sent out to the provinces monitored official conduct and reported corruption, institutionalising accountability within the bureaucratic hierarchy. This combination of ideological training, administrative codification, and internal supervision created a governing apparatus capable of managing some 60 million people across diverse ecological and cultural zones with a coherence no prior Chinese state had achieved.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#rome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rome.jpg" alt="Rome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trevi_Fountain,_Rome,_Italy_2_-_May_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diliff, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Han Dynasty's most consequential contribution to world history was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a>, the network of overland and maritime trade routes connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, opened by Zhang Qian's missions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>'s western campaigns and sustained by Han military presence across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>. Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lacquerware">lacquerware</a>, and iron goods moved westward while glass, gold, and horses moved east, creating an unprecedented sustained exchange of goods, technologies, and ideas across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eurasia">Eurasia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buddhism">Buddhism</a> entered China along these routes during the Eastern Han, initiating the most significant religious transformation in Chinese cultural history, and Han-era advances such as papermaking, refined by Cai Lun in 105 CE, later spread across the world. The Han model of Confucian imperial governance shaped the administrative traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vietnam">Vietnam</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#japan">Japan</a> for centuries. Chinese identity itself was consolidated during the Han period to such a degree that the majority ethnic group of China calls itself Han to this day.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#yellow-turban-rebellion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yellow-turban-rebellion.jpg" alt="Yellow Turban Rebellion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#yellow-turban-rebellion">Yellow Turban Rebellion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yellow_Turban_Rebellion.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SY, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Han decline was driven by the structural contradiction between an imperial system requiring strong central authority and a landed aristocracy progressively consolidating control over land and local governance. Large landowners evaded taxation by absorbing peasant smallholders into dependent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tenancy">tenancy</a>, eroding the tax base while concentrating private military capacity outside state control, while bitter feuds between court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eunuch">eunuchs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a> paralysed the court. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yellow-turban-rebellion">Yellow Turban Rebellion</a> of 184 CE, a millenarian Taoist uprising fed by decades of peasant displacement and famine, forced the court to delegate military authority to regional commanders whose loyalty was personal rather than institutional. After Dong Zhuo seized the capital in 189, the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> controlled the last Han emperor from 196 CE onwards, and the dynasty ended in 220 CE when his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cao-pi">Cao Pi</a> deposed Emperor Xian and declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cao-wei">Wei dynasty</a>, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> was briefly reunified under the Jin.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Han Dynasty's historical significance lies not in its territorial extent alone but in its creation of a governing ideology, a bureaucratic architecture, and a cultural identity so durable that they defined the meaning of Chinese civilisation for every dynasty that followed across two thousand years.</em></p>

